-- Regression: a fixity-declared operator chain must re-associate whether its
-- operands are literals or bare identifiers. Identifier operands parse to a
-- DIFFERENT flat shape than literals — `a OP b OP c` becomes an operand-head
-- App `App a [Var OP, b, Var OP, c]` (juxtaposition), not the operator-head
-- `App OP [a, b, Var OP, c]` that literals produce. Re-association must handle
-- both. (The original fixity implementation only handled the operator-head
-- shape, so chains over identifier operands — the common case for named
-- values, and for the prelude's own set operators — silently failed to
-- re-associate.)

@infixl 6
GIVEN p IS A NUMBER
      q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
p joinL q MEANS p + q

@infixl 7
GIVEN p IS A NUMBER
      q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
p meetL q MEANS p * q

GIVETH A NUMBER
aa MEANS 1

GIVETH A NUMBER
bb MEANS 2

GIVETH A NUMBER
cc MEANS 3

-- identifier operands, same operator: (aa joinL bb) joinL cc = 6
#EVAL aa joinL bb joinL cc

-- identifier operands, mixed precedence: aa joinL (bb meetL cc) = 1 + 6 = 7
#EVAL aa joinL bb meetL cc

-- parenthesized controls agree
#EVAL (aa joinL bb) joinL cc
#EVAL aa joinL (bb meetL cc)

-- the tighter grouping is a different value, reachable only with parens
#EVAL (aa joinL bb) meetL cc

-- mixing identifier and literal operands in one chain
#EVAL aa joinL 2 meetL cc
